NAME
     man - the manual

SYNOPSIS
     man [NAME...]
     man -k WORD...

DESCRIPTION
     man prints the manual page for each NAME. With no arguments it
     prints the index: every page, with the one line that says what it
     is. With -k it prints the lines of that index which mention any of
     the given words, which is how to find a page whose name you do not
     know.

     The pages live in one file, /usr/share/man/man.db, written at build
     time from man/NAME.1 in the source tree. Every utility in this
     system has a page, and the C library answers `NAME --help' from the
     same file (the NAME, SYNOPSIS and OPTIONS sections of it), so the
     two cannot disagree.

OPTIONS
     -k   search the index instead of printing a page

EXAMPLES
     man vmm            what the hypervisor's front end does
     man -k namespace   every page that mentions namespaces
     man                everything there is

SEE ALSO
     which(1)
